{
  "_note": "SF Symbol name -> cross-platform icon resolution. THE #1 .dsx portability seam: icon=/systemImage=/tabIcon=/actionIcon= (and the snapshot dialect's symbol=) name SF Symbols, which exist only on Apple platforms (and may not be redistributed). iOS renders the SF name directly (the fast path); every other runtime resolves it HERE. Per entry: `material` = the Material Symbols name (Apache-2.0, google/material-design-icons), `codepoint` = that glyph's hex codepoint in the Material Symbols font (the PUA cmap slot - render the char at this codepoint with the bundled font), `fallback` = a plain unicode/text stand-in for when no icon font is available (best-effort; a renderer with neither font nor fallback draws its placeholder). Fail-open: an SF name not in this table renders the consumer's placeholder, never an error. Keyed by the SF name because the .dsx corpus is SF-named (StackReference.md: the icon attribute is a semantic token resolved by the same name on every platform - never a per-platform id). COVERAGE: every distinct icon=/symbol= literal in the shipped .dsx corpus (ClosedSource/DSX/Modules, incl. ternary/default-expression literals) + the StackReference.md defaults (EmptyState `tray`, the tab examples). Adding an icon to markup? Add its row here in the same change - Android consumes this file as a packaged :render asset (see OpenSource/Engine/Android/render/build.gradle.kts copySfMapJson; ONE file, never forked).",
  "_fill_axis": "SF `.fill` variants map to the SAME material name as their outline sibling: Material Symbols express fill as a variable-font FILL axis, and the bundled Android subset is instanced at the default FILL=0 (outline) look. A filled-vs-outline pair that must stay visually distinct (selection states) maps to two DIFFERENT material glyphs instead - see circle -> radio_button_unchecked vs circle.fill -> fiber_manual_record.",
  "_web_axis": "`web` = the row's 24x24 SVG path `d` for renderers that have neither the Material Symbols font nor SF Symbols - the browsers. It is the WEB render ladder's rung 1 (OpenSource/Web/packages/dom/src/elements.ts iconSvg): `web` path -> `fallback` unicode text -> the consumer's placeholder, the same fail-open shape as the Android ladder. The tier is a UNIFORM stroke tier (viewBox 0 0 24 24, fill none, stroke currentColor, stroke-width 2, round caps/joins), so - exactly like `_fill_axis` above - an SF `.fill` variant carries the SAME path as its outline sibling unless the pair must stay visually distinct (circle vs circle.fill). A row MAY omit `web`: that row then renders its `fallback` glyph on web, which is what `fallback` is for. Rows deliberately left on the fallback rung are the pictographic ones whose brand/organic shape a 2px stroke cannot honestly carry (apple.logo, crown.fill, flame.fill, pianokeys, puzzlepiece.extension, tuningfork, recordingtape, books.vertical, bitcoinsign.circle.fill, metronome) - a documented divergence, not silent breakage. THIS FILE IS THE ONLY COPY: OpenSource/Web generates packages/dom/src/icons.generated.ts from it (npm run icons:generate) and packages/dom/test/icons.test.ts FAILS when the generated table drifts from this file or when any row here cannot be drawn on web.",
  "_subset_provenance": "The bundled Android asset (OpenSource/Engine/Android/render/src/main/assets/despia/icons/MaterialSymbolsOutlined-subset.ttf) is generated FROM google/material-design-icons variablefont/MaterialSymbolsOutlined[FILL,GRAD,opsz,wght].ttf (name-table Version 2.962, Apache-2.0) BY: fontTools varLib.instancer at the default axes (wght=400 FILL=0 GRAD=0 opsz=24) then pyftsubset --unicodes=<every `codepoint` in this file>. Regenerate the subset in the SAME change as any row addition; :render's FontSubsetTest fails the build when a row's codepoint is missing from the shipped cmap.",
  "_license": "Material Symbols (google/material-design-icons) is Apache License 2.0 - redistribution of the font subset + this name/codepoint table is permitted with attribution; the license text ships next to the bundled font (OpenSource/Engine/Android/render/src/main/assets/despia/icons/).",
